r"""
CHAIN-004 - A delegated help-desk role removes the workspace's administrators,
            then breaks the workspace in a way only they could have repaired

Steps (each proven on its own in stage 11; this script runs them as one attack):
  1. VULN-020  roles.addUserToRole answers error-user-already-in-role before the
               permission check, so any account can ask "does this user hold the
               admin role?" and read the answer from the error code.
               -> the complete list of the workspace's administrators, from an
                  account that holds no permission to read roles.
  2. VULN-069  users.update accepts a roles array that omits admin with only
               assign-roles plus edit-other-user-info, while the promotion side
               is guarded by assign-admin-role.
               -> every administrator demoted to an ordinary user, one call each,
                  with no way back: the same caller cannot grant the role.
  3. VULN-057  statistics.telemetry increments any numeric setting for any
               authenticated caller, Accounts_Password_Policy_MinLength included.
               -> an authentication policy nobody can change back, because step 2
                  removed everyone who could.

Vulnerable code:
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/api/v1/roles.ts:129-146 (the
      already-in-role answer precedes the permission check)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/api/v1/users.ts (users.update: the role
      list is replaced with no assign-admin-role check and no last-admin guard)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/lib/statistics/functions/updateStatsCounter.ts:10
      (increments the settings id the caller names)

The chain
---------
Rocket.Chat is designed to let an administrator delegate user management without
giving away the workspace: a role gets assign-roles and edit-other-user-info,
and assign-admin-role is deliberately withheld. This chain shows the delegation
is one-directional in the wrong direction. The oracle names the administrators
without any permission to read roles; the role-write hole removes them all; and
once no administrator exists, the telemetry settings write does damage that, by
construction, nobody left in the workspace can undo. The attacker cannot promote
itself (the guard holds), so the end state is not takeover but seizure: an
ownerless workspace with a policy that refuses every registration, password
change and password reset.

Lab safety: a spare administrator is created by this run before anything else and
is the only administrator left alive, so the workspace can be put back exactly as
it was. The run demotes the workspace's real administrator account, proves that
account can no longer repair the damage, and then restores it from the spare and
deletes everything it created. The final administrator is removable by the
identical call; the run does not do it, because that would leave this lab with no
administrator at all.

What a successful run proves
---------------------------
A help-desk role with no admin rights enumerated the administrators, demoted the
workspace's own administrator account (which then could not read or write a
protected setting), raised the password policy so that registration and every
password change fail workspace-wide, and the demoted administrator was refused
when it tried to put the setting back.

Dependencies: requests.
"""
